window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_physics_unitsandmeasurements=[
{"id":"neet-2026r-q002","pn":2,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Units and Measurements","st":"Vernier calliper zero error","q":"One main scale division of a Vernier calliper is equal to 1 mm and the number of divisions on the Vernier scale is 10. When both the jaws touch each other, the Vernier scale shifts to the left of zero of the main scale in such a way that 4th Vernier division coincides with a division of the main scale. If this Vernier calliper measures the length of the wire to be 1 cm, the actual length of the wire is :","o":["0.96 cm","1.00 cm","1.04 cm","0.60 cm"],"a":"No option is correct","s":"The Vernier scale is to the left of zero, so there is a negative zero error.\nZero error = –(10 – 4) × L.C. (L.C. = 0.01 cm).\nZero error = –(10 – 4) × 0.01 = –0.06 cm.\nZero correction = – Zero error = –(–0.06) = 0.06 cm.\nCorrect reading R′ = R + zero correction = 1 + 0.06 = 1.06 cm.\nNo option gives 1.06 cm, so no option is correct.\nNote: Printed answer: No option is correct (working gives 1.06 cm)"},
{"id":"neet-2026-q004","pn":4,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Units and Measurements","st":"Changing system of units","q":"The speed of light in vacuum is taken as unity. If light takes 6 min 40 s to reach the Earth from the Sun, the distance between the Sun and the Earth in new unit is :","o":["3 × 10⁸","3 × 10¹⁰","400","500"],"c":2,"s":"Time t = 6 min 40 s = 360 + 40 = 400 s.\nDistance d = vt.\nWith v = 1 in the new system, d = 1 × 400 = 400."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q006","pn":6,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Units and Measurements","st":"Vernier callipers least count","q":"In a vernier callipers, 20 VSD coincide with 16 MSD (each division of length 1 mm). The least count of the vernier callipers is-","o":["0.01 cm","0.1 cm","0.02 cm","0.2 cm"],"c":2,"s":"Least count L.C. = 1 MSD − 1 VSD ... (i)\n20 VSD = 16 MSD, so 1 VSD = (16)/(20) MSD.\nFrom (i): L.C. = 1 MSD − (16)/(20) MSD = (4)/(20) MSD.\nL.C. = (1)/(5) mm = 0.2 mm.\nL.C. = 0.02 cm."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q009","pn":9,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Units and Measurements","st":"Dimensions of constants","q":"Consider that σₛ, k_B, b represents Stefan-Boltzmann constant, Boltzmann constant and Wien’s displacement law constant, respectively. The dimension of σₛ k_B⁻¹ b is","o":["[L⁻¹K⁻²]","[L⁻¹T⁻³K⁻³]","[L⁻¹T⁻¹K⁻⁴]","[L⁻¹T⁻¹K⁻²]"],"c":3,"s":"σₛ = MT⁻³K⁻⁴.\nk_B = M¹L²T⁻²K⁻¹.\nb = M⁰L¹T⁰K¹.\nσₛ k_B⁻¹ b = [MT⁻³K⁻⁴][M⁻¹L⁻²T²K][M⁰LT⁰K].\n= T⁻¹K⁻²L⁻¹.\n= L⁻¹T⁻¹K⁻²."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q021","pn":21,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Units and Measurements","st":"Significant figures","q":"Each side of a metallic cube of mass 5.580 kg is measured to be 9.0 cm. Keeping the significant figures in view, the density of the material of the cube can be best expressed as X × 10³ kg m⁻³ where the value of X is:","o":["7.654","7.7","7.65","7.6"],"c":1,"s":"Density = Mass/Volume = 5.580 kg/(9.0 cm)³.\n= (5.580/729) × 10⁶ kg/m³.\n= 0.007654321 × 10⁶ kg/m³.\n= 7.654 × 10³ kg/m³.\nThe side 9.0 cm has only 2 significant figures, so the answer is 7.7 × 10³ kg/m³.\nHence X = 7.7."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q032","pn":32,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Units and Measurements","st":"Simple pendulum experiment","q":"Savitha, a XI standard student, while conducting an experiment to determine the effective length of a simple pendulum L, notes down the data of time taken to complete 30 oscillations as 60 s and hence calculates the length of the simple pendulum as:\n(Take π² = 9.8, and g = 9.8 m/s²)","o":["2 m","0.75 m","1.5 m","1 m"],"c":3,"s":"Time for 30 oscillations = 60 s.\nTime period = time for 1 oscillation, so T = 60/30 = 2 s.\nT = 2π√(ℓ/g) gives ℓ = gT²/4π².\nℓ = (9.8 × 2²)/(4 × 9.8) = 1 m."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q024","pn":24,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Units and Measurements","st":"Dimensional analysis","q":"A balloon is made of a material of surface tension S and its inflation outlet (from where gas is filled in it) has small area A. It is filled with a gas of density ρ and takes a spherical shape of radius R. When the gas is allowed to flow freely out of it, its radius r changes from R to 0 (zero) in time T. If the speed v(r) of gas coming out of the balloon depends on r as rᵃ and T ∝ Sᵅ Aᵝ ργ Rδ then","o":["a = 1/2, α = 1/2, β = −1, γ = +1, δ = 3/2","a = −1/2, α = −1/2, β = −1, γ = −1/2, δ = 5/2","a = −1/2, α = −1/2, β = −1, γ = 1/2, δ = 7/2","a = 1/2, α = 1/2, β = −1/2, γ = 1/2, δ = 7/2"],"c":2,"s":"T ∝ SᵅAᵝργRδ\nM⁰L⁰T¹ = K (MT⁻²)ᵅ (L²)ᵝ (ML⁻³)ᵞ Lδ\nM⁰L⁰T¹ = K [M^(α+γ) L^(2β−3γ+δ) T^(−2α)]\n−2α = 1, so α = −1/2\nα + γ = 0, so γ = 1/2\n2β − 3γ + δ = 0\n2β − 3(1/2) + δ = 0\nBy hit and trial (using option (1)), put β = −1:\n2(−1) − 3/2 + δ = 0, so δ = 7/2\nNote: the solution says 'using option (1)' for the trial of β = −1, but the matching printed option is (3); the exponent 'a' of v(r) is not worked out in the solution"},
{"id":"neet-2025-q025","pn":25,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Units and Measurements","st":"Vernier callipers, zero error","q":"Consider the diameter of a spherical object being measured with the help of a Vernier callipers. Suppose its 10 Vernier Scale Divisions (V.S.D.) are equal to its 9 Main Scale Divisions (M.S.D.). The least division in the M.S. is 0.1 cm and the zero of V.S. is at x = 0.1 cm when the jaws of Vernier callipers are closed.\nIf the main scale reading for the diameter is M = 5 cm and the number of coinciding vernier division is 8, the measured diameter after zero error correction, is","o":["5.18 cm","5.08 cm","4.98 cm","5.00 cm"],"c":2,"s":"Least count = 1 MSD − 1 VSD = 1 MSD − (9/10) MSD = (1/10) MSD\nLeast count = (1/10) × 0.1 cm = 0.01 cm\nZero error = +0.1 cm\nMain scale reading = 5 cm\nVernier scale reading = 8 × 0.01 = 0.08 cm\nFinal diameter = 5 + 0.08 − 0.1 = 4.98 cm"},
{"id":"neet-2025-q042","pn":42,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Units and Measurements","st":"Percentage error","q":"A physical quantity P is related to four observations a, b, c and d as follows:\nP = a³b² / c√d\nThe percentage errors of measurement in a, b, c and d are 1%, 3%, 2%, and 4% respectively. The percentage error in the quantity P is","o":["10%","2%","13%","15%"],"c":2,"s":"Maximum % error in P = (ΔP/P) × 100 = 3(Δa/a × 100) + 2(Δb/b × 100) + (Δc/c × 100) + (1/2)(Δd/d × 100).\n= 3 × (1) + 2 × (3) + (2) + (1/2) × (4)\n= 13%"},
{"id":"neet-2024-q007","pn":7,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Units and Measurements","st":"Vernier constant","q":"In a vernier calipers, (N + 1) divisions of vernier scale coincide with N divisions of main scale. If 1 MSD represent 0.1 mm, the vernier constant (in cm) is-","o":["100 N","10(N + 1)","1/(10 N)","1/(100(N + 1))"],"c":3,"s":"Vernier constant V.C = MSD – VSD   ...(1)\nGiven (N + 1) VSD = N MSD, so VSD = (N/(N + 1)) MSD   ...(2)\nFrom (1) and (2): V.C = MSD – (N/(N + 1)) MSD = MSD (1 – N/(N + 1)) = MSD/(N + 1).\n1 MSD = 0.1 mm = 0.01 cm.\nSo V.C = 0.01/(N + 1) = 1/(100(N + 1)) cm."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q032","pn":32,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Units and Measurements","st":"Dimensions of dimensionless quantities","q":"The quantities which have the same dimensions as those of solid angle are;","o":["strain and arc","angular speed and stress","strain and angle","stress and angle"],"c":2,"s":"Solid angle dΩ = dA/r² has dimensions [M⁰L⁰T⁰].\nStrain = Δℓ/ℓ has dimensions [M⁰L⁰T⁰].\nAn angle in radians, θ = ℓ/r, is also dimensionless [M⁰L⁰T⁰]."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q041","pn":41,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Units and Measurements","st":"Dimensional analysis","q":"A force defined by F = αt² + βt acts on a particle at a given time t. The factor which is dimensionless, if α and β are constants, is:","o":["αβt","αβ/t","βt/α","αt/β"],"c":3,"s":"By the principle of homogeneity, [F] = [αt²] = [βt].\nSo [α] = [F]/[t²] and [β] = [F]/[t].\nThen [α][t] = [β].\nTherefore αt/β is dimensionless."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q009","pn":9,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Units and Measurements","st":"Error propagation","q":"A metal wire has mass (0.4 ± 0.002) g, radius (0.3 ± 0.001) mm and length (5 ± 0.02) cm. The maximum possible percentage error in the measurement of density will nearly be:","o":["1.4%","1.2%","1.3%","1.6%"],"c":3,"s":"Density ρ = M/(πr²ℓ).\nMaximum fractional error: Δρ/ρ = ΔM/M + 2Δr/r + Δℓ/ℓ.\n= 0.002/0.4 + 2 × 0.001/0.3 + 0.02/5.\n= 0.0156.\nPercentage error = 1.56% ≈ 1.6%."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q011","pn":11,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Units and Measurements","st":"Types of errors","q":"The errors in the measurement which arise due to unpredictable fluctuations in temperature and voltage supply are:","o":["Random errors","Instrumental errors","Personal errors","Least count errors"],"c":0,"s":"Errors that have no systematic or constant cause are random errors.\nThey arise from unpredictable changes in conditions, such as temperature, pressure or voltage supply."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q022","pn":22,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Units and Measurements","st":"Dimensions of angle","q":"Plane angle and solid angle have","o":["Dimensions but no units","No units and no dimensions","Both units and dimensions","Units but no dimensions"],"c":3,"s":"Plane angle = arc/radius = [L]/[L] = [M⁰L⁰T⁰]; its unit is radian.\nSolid angle = area/(radius)² = L²/L² = [M⁰L⁰T⁰]; its unit is steradian.\nSo both have units but no dimensions."},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q028","pn":28,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Units and Measurements","st":"Dimensional formula","q":"The physical quantity that has the same dimensional formula as pressure is:","o":["Coefficient of viscosity","Force","Momentum","Young's modulus of elasticity"],"c":3,"s":"Pressure = F/A, so [P] = MLT⁻²/L² = ML⁻¹T⁻².\nYoung's modulus = stress/strain. Strain has no dimension, so [Y] = ML⁻¹T⁻².\nThus Young's modulus has the same dimensions as pressure."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q032","pn":32,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Units and Measurements","st":"Dimensions of physical quantities","q":"The dimensions [MLT⁻²A⁻²] belong to the","o":["Self inductance","Magnetic permeability","Electric permittivity","Magnetic flux"],"c":1,"s":"The dimensional formula of magnetic permeability is [MLT⁻²A⁻²]."},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q037","pn":37,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Units and Measurements","st":"Percentage error","q":"The percentage error in the measurement of g is: (Given that g = 4π²L/T², L = (10 ± 0.1) cm, T = (100 ± 1)s)","o":["7%","2%","5%","3%"],"c":3,"s":"Δg/g × 100 = ΔL/L × 100 + 2 ΔT/T × 100.\nΔL/L × 100 = 0.1/10 × 100 = 1%.\n2 ΔT/T × 100 = 2 × 1/100 × 100 = 2%.\nTotal = 1 + 2 = 3%."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q041","pn":41,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Units and Measurements","st":"Significant figures","q":"The area of a rectangular field (in m²) of length 55.3 m and breadth 25 m after rounding off the value for correct significant digits is","o":["1382","1382.5","14 × 10²","138 × 10¹"],"c":2,"s":"Area = 55.3 × 25 = 1382.5 m².\nBreadth 25 has two significant figures, so the answer keeps two.\nRounded value = 14 × 10² m²."},
{"id":"neet-2021-q007","pn":7,"y":"NEET 2021 · 12 Sep","tp":"Units and Measurements","st":"Dimensional formula","q":"If E and G respectively denote energy and gravitational constant, then E/G has the dimensions of","o":["[M²][L⁻²][T⁻¹]","[M²][L⁻¹][T⁰]","[M][L⁻¹][T⁻¹]","[M][L⁰][T⁰]"],"c":1,"s":"[E] = [M L² T⁻²].\n[G] = [M⁻¹ L³ T⁻²].\nDivide: [M¹⁻⁽⁻¹⁾ L²⁻³ T⁻²⁻⁽⁻²⁾] = [M² L⁻¹ T⁰].\nSo option (2)."},
{"id":"neet-2021-q015","pn":15,"y":"NEET 2021 · 12 Sep","tp":"Units and Measurements","st":"Screw gauge","q":"A screw gauge gives the following readings when used to measure the diameter of a wire\nMain scale reading : 0 mm Circular scale reading : 52 divisions\nGiven that 1 mm on main scale corresponds to 100 divisions on the circular scale. The diameter of the wire from the above data is","o":["0.052 cm","0.52 cm","0.026 cm","0.26 cm"],"c":0,"s":"Pitch = 1 mm for 100 divisions.\nLeast count = 1/100 mm = 0.01 mm = 0.001 cm.\nDiameter = 0 + 52 × 0.001 cm = 0.052 cm."},
{"id":"neet-2021-q029","pn":29,"y":"NEET 2021 · 12 Sep","tp":"Units and Measurements","st":"Dimensional analysis","q":"If force [F], acceleration [A] and time [T] are chosen as the fundamental physical quantities. Find the dimensions of energy.","o":["[F][A⁻¹][T]","[F][A][T]","[F][A][T²]","[F][A][T⁻¹]"],"c":2,"s":"Let E = Fᵃ Aᵇ Tᶜ.\n[E] = [M L² T⁻²], [F] = [M L T⁻²], [A] = [L T⁻²].\nRight side = M^a L^(a+b) T^(–2a–2b+c).\nMass: a = 1.\nLength: a + b = 2, so b = 1.\nTime: –2a – 2b + c = –2 gives –4 + c = –2, so c = 2.\nE = [F][A][T²]."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q100","pn":100,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Units and Measurements","st":"Screw gauge","q":"A screw gauge has least count of 0.01 mm and there are 50 divisions in its circular scale. The pitch of the screw gauge is :","o":["0.25 mm","0.5 mm","1.0 mm","0.01 mm"],"c":1,"s":"Least count = pitch / number of circular divisions.\n0.01 mm = pitch/50.\nPitch = 0.01 × 50 = 0.5 mm."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q105","pn":105,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Units and Measurements","st":"Significant figures","q":"Taking into account of the significant figures, what is the value of 9.99 m – 0.0099 m ?","o":["9.98 m","9.980 m","9.9 m","9.9801 m"],"c":0,"s":"9.99 − 0.0099 = 9.9801.\nIn subtraction the result keeps as many decimal places as the term with fewer decimal places.\n9.99 has 2 decimal places, so the answer is 9.98 m."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q124","pn":124,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Units and Measurements","st":"Dimensions","q":"Dimensions of stress are :","o":["[ML²T⁻²]","[ML⁰T⁻²]","[ML⁻¹T⁻²]","[MLT⁻²]"],"c":2,"s":"Stress = force/area.\nForce = [MLT⁻²], area = [L²].\nStress = [MLT⁻²]/[L²] = [ML⁻¹T⁻²]."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q131","pn":131,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Units and Measurements","st":"Unit conversion","q":"The energy required to break one bond in DNA is 10⁻²⁰ J. This value in eV is nearly :","o":["0.6","0.06","0.006","6"],"c":1,"s":"1 eV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.\nE = 10⁻²⁰/(1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹) eV.\n= 0.625 × 10⁻¹ = 0.0625 eV ≈ 0.06 eV."}
];
